From 18027513eafa294663fa3eab33661e9580ce2c1b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 29 Sep 2026 08:16:47 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 119/119] DJ: a DJ filter on the song's gold knobs, sync to the
 tempo heard, a nudge that bends the internal clock

The first step of the DJ tool (research/DJ-TOOL-PLAN.md, v19.0): the Deluge played along with an external player.

DJ filter (dsp/dj/dj_filter.h): the song's filter type DJ, after LPF, HPF and EQ when the upper gold knob is pressed
in Song view (AFFECT ENTIRE, the filter row); clips and sounds go round as before. The upper knob drives the song's
own LPF and HPF cutoff params as the gold knobs set them (automation recorded where it is being recorded): from -64
(the low-pass closed) through the middle (both off) to +64 (the high-pass open). Left of the middle the low-pass
closes over its whole range, two of its steps a detent, with the high-pass off; right of it the high-pass opens with
the low-pass off. Where the two were set apart (both on, in LPF and HPF mode or by automation) a detent goes on from
there without a jump: to the right the low-pass opens until it is off, then the high-pass opens; to the left the
high-pass closes, then the low-pass. The lower knob turns both resonances, each from where it is, and shows the one
of the filter that is on. Popups "DJ: LPF 16", "DJ: OFF", "DJ: HPF 16" (7 segments LP16, OFF, HP16). Saved as
currentFilterType="dj"; a firmware without it loads LPF there. The filters themselves are unchanged.

Sync (Scan view): TAP TEMPO sets the song's tempo to the BPM shown (halved or doubled as the horizontal encoder
chose), undoable, "Sync 128.0 BPM"; "No tempo yet" before a tempo is heard, "External clock" where an external clock
sets the tempo. SHIFT + TAP TEMPO: the metronome, as everywhere.

Nudge (PlaybackHandler::nudgeInternalClock()): the horizontal encoder held and the tempo encoder turned nudged only
the MIDI clock out in 1.2.1 (a clock sent extra or skipped), and on the internal clock without MIDI clock out it did
nothing. Now it bends the Deluge's own clock as a jog wheel bends a record: each detent makes the time per timer tick
4 % shorter (right) or longer (left) for 0.25 s after it, which moves the beat by about 10 ms at any tempo; turning
on keeps it bent. The clock outputs (MIDI, trigger) follow, as setTimePerTimerTick() reschedules them. After it the
tempo is exactly what it was, unless it was set meanwhile; STOP ends a bend at once; a song saved while bent saves
its tempo without the bend. SHIFT keeps 1.2.1's nudge of the MIDI clock out alone. An external clock: as before.

tests/dj/run.sh (PC, UBSan): the knob's curve, a detent at all 129 positions, and no jump from any LPF and HPF knob
(33,282 starts). tests/dj/dj_emu.py drives it all on the whole firmware in the emulator (26 checks): the filter's
positions and popups, set apart and taken on from there, the resonances; sync to 128.0000 BPM; a nudge 215.33 ->
206.72 samples a tick, back exactly 0.3 s later with the beat 460 samples (10.4 ms) ahead, 423 behind the other way,
and STOP at once puts the tempo back.

